aning i thought the whole united  states army was like trieste but what  what it was italy and trieste in italy  yeah it was called trust united states  troops tries to united states troops and  uh when i left that command i was well  trained in the basics and my platoon  sergeant would  beat it into our heads if you don't  learn it right you won't do it right and  we learned it right by repetition by  iron discipline  and by great motivation great spirit so  i took those values and for the next 21  years imposed them on any unit that i uh  served in and i was always in trouble  for being too tough for being to to  discipline but i'll tell you one thing  brian very few of my soldiers died  because i made damn certain they were  well trained and if you're going to have  an army then that's their function you  got to make certain they're well trained  if you have an electrical company that  that that's playing with electricity you  want to make damn well sure that all  your electricians have played with  electricity before and know that if they  touch the wrong thing they're dead how  much formal education do you have  seventh grade  at that time seventh grade yes how about  now uh university degree the the army  was incredibly good to me uh they  educated me they sent me to university i  went to austin p university in in uh  tennessee and uh got a degree in history  and and so  i'm not just knocking the army in terms  of  attacking an institution i i have a  great love for this institution because  it  it was my home it looked after me and i  just want to make sure that we'd never  repeat the mistakes of the past i think  the united states army will ever fight  again in a conventional war  well that's hard to say and i don't have  a crystal ball i don't think that i  don't think you'll see a war between  superpowers i think that both the soviet  union and the united states realize that  that the destructive powers that they  they have at the end of their cannons is  so so mind-boggling that it would  destroy uh destroy the their countries  and to destroy the world somebody told  me but i do think if i just  finish that i do think that we will see  wars of national liberation similar to  what we had in vietnam i think we'll see  wars in latin america latin america is  exploding and i think that that that is  where our strategic interest was really  threatened and i think that we need  forces who know how to deal with that  and more important we need diplomats  right here in your city who can solve  the matter without conflict who can  solve the matter through  programs of education and and  stopping this whole gringo thing of the  of the arrogant yank coming in and and  uh uh  taking advantage of somebody that  doesn't have the good things that we  have i think that we there a lot can be  done  uh in latin america to preclude a war  but i think we've got to be prepared for  that contingency how long were you in  korea  i was there in 1950 51 52 i went to fort  benning for uh in 1952  beginning  yeah just kind of like a mid-level  chorus on infantry which went from the  company to regiment and then i went back  to korea 52-53 how long were we in  vietnam 65 through  all of 65-66  60 part of 67 then 69 70 and half of 71.  what did we not learn from korea or  there's probably a better way to ask  that but what mistakes do we make in  korea well that's a very very good  question because  in korea  we made a lot of a lot of mistakes we  simply we went there with the view that  well here comes uncle sam we've just  kicked the japs and the germans tale and  we anybody gets in our way uh it was  like john wayne and top gun type thing  uh better stand aside and we thought our  presence which would stop the north  koreans as we did the same in vietnam as  we did recently in lebanon our presence  would would cool things off and uh  we sent an army that was badly trained  badly led by a lot of tired old men and  badly equipped that hadn't been trained  for the battlefield and we made a lot of  mistakes and the things didn't turn  around until we got a leader ridgeway  and when general walker was killed  ridgeway took over and he turned one in  one of the most  important  events of military history how one man  can take a defeated army and turn it  around that's why leadership is threaded  through this discussion so much if you  got the right leader you can get the job  done go back to general ridgeway though  and just tell me what you liked about  him as a leader he was on the  battlefield he knew his trade  meaning he knew what what the tactics  were about he knew what strategy was  about and he was not afraid afraid to  tell the president of the united states  truman at that time when things were not  going right did you know him i never  knew him personally but i've admired him  and i served under his command and of  course in the 8th army how many people  did you have command over in korea  in korea or the most would be a rifle  company a couple hundred men  and what technique did you use  leadership technique did you use in  korea that you can remember that people  responded to  i talked to my soldiers i was there i  would never give an order that  that that i wouldn't do myself and and i  love my soldiers i never wasted them  they knew that and  we  we  formed a very perfect team i was very  hard on them meaning i was like a father  that  that uh  uh took his children and and laid a uh  a very disciplined trip on him but we  were not playing in the backyard we were  playing in a life and death game and if  i could train them where they were  competent and confident then i knew they  could make it through the night into the  most horrible experience that a human  can endure and that's being an  infantryman on the battlefield when  shells are crashing in booby traps are  exploding machine guns are ripping away  so it's it's a it's a one big raging  atrocity brian  based on what your experience was  did you ever change the way you led  troops after korea i always led from the  front and and that was ridgeway's way of  leading and physically go to the front  physically ahead of them oh well  i  uh  in  the the delta of vietnam when i took  over battalion that was so badly trained  it was filled with draftees and bad  leadership and so on that i virtually  had to become a squad leader again  meaning i virtually had to lead from the  front but as soon as i got him trained  up i got back to where i could command  you know you really can't uh fight  a battalion or a brigade by playing  squad leader you've got to get back and  look at the battle and see it see it  from an overview point but there's  always a time that you've got to go and  understand the pulse of it you've just  got to grab onto the pulse of the fight  and to find out what's going on the  great generals of history the the  jacksons and the rommels and the  ridgeways and the gavins and so on were  guys who led from the front not from  their helicopter as was in vietnam or  not from the command-and-control bunker  that's in the pentagon they were there  and they knew what was going on and they  had a good feel for the war another man  who was outspoken in vietnam  who has been now the subject of another  very successful book neil shins  bright shining light john paul vann  got out as lieutenant colonel came back  as a major general in the civilian world  well he knew him well he was my company  commander in korea for a while and i  worked with him in the delta and i as a  guy i admired a great deal why  he was one of the kind of guys that we  were talking about he wasn't afraid to  stand up and tell the truth he flew  around one of those planes oh yeah but  uh  that's uh when he was was killed uh and  i flew around one of those planes too  but i wasn't reluctant to land the damn  thing and get on the ground and leave my  troops nor was john paul van he was a  doer a shaker and an and a a maker and i  i liked him and they i thought he was a  good man neil sheen gives you a mixed  picture though on him well neil shannon  goes into his uh  personal background and uh  i don't know if i cared for all of that  but i didn't write the book that's up  for to neil sheehan to say what he had  to say but i think i enjoyed the book  very much and i think that our book  certainly dovetails very neatly with it  as a companion piece  because we're talking about the war from  the standpoint of the u.s where that was  the vietnamese side even though we have  one strong chapter that deals with arvin  and the vietnamese and and  from my almost uh two plus years  experience working as an advisor at  brigade and division and special zone  level in vietnam with the vietnamese  you  what do you think men in  the military in a war situation respond  to  the best personal relationship tough  versus  loud versus quiet all those little  things that people study when it comes  to leadership what was your style did  you scream and yell at them no i don't  think a lot done by screaming and  yelling i think that  by setting the example  by  winning their confidence letting them  know that you're not a butcher that you  love them that you're going to care for  them and uh  uh and that you really know your trade  and once your soldiers know that okay  and i'll give you a good story that that  that's told in and about faces is i  taken over this battalion this one i  described to draftees and on the first  day and i just couldn't believe the  condition of this unit was just total  disintegration and i  uh  reckoned that their defensive positions  were wouldn't hack it if we were tapped  if the enemy hit us uh we'd we'd be in  serious trouble so my first order after  taking over the unit was  by midnight i want us to be fall fall  back 300 yards i want new positions dug  and and i went all this junk taken out  there was there was uh  cokes and and uh soft drinks and little  containers on position the men slept in  cots  the weapons were rusty ammunition was in  the mud it was just simply not a  professional military organization it  was an organization that had been  blooded for six months and they were  just waiting for a disaster so i fell  them back they dug in their nose and  their their new holes got their new  positions together with a lot of  grumbling who is this new guy that they  would call me gi joe who's gonna  change this thing around and blow our  nice soft life apart and uh the uh  exactly at midnight that night as if i  would have planned it we got hit with  everything that the enemy could throw at  us with rockets and with artillery and  fire of all sorts and the men went to  their defensive position and now i had  been away from troops uh  from combat troops okay i had a  battalion at fort  lewis  just before i came to vietnam on this  tour but i'd been away from the war for  a couple years and and i was nervous as  nelly you know i was grabbed the radio i  thought geez i hope i don't blow this  thing here's with all my combat  experience so you can imagine these  dilettantes that came in who hadn't been  with troops for 10 or 15 years and took  a battalion okay  and  as i was  adjusting artillery and calling in air  and getting flare ships and all these  good things you do and under those  circumstances a young lieutenant ran by  to go into his fighting position and he  said i'll tell you what he's a mean son  of a [ __ ] but he knows what he's doing  and that that was success and that you  know yeah and the next day the grumbling  slowed down and that those words were  probably spread throughout the battalion  that i knew what i was doing and if we  had more people  in vietnam that knew what they were  doing  i think the outcome of that war at the  tactical level on the battlefield would  have been a lot different we would have  come out with with real honor rather  than the the kissinger peace with honor  meaning where we sold out our allies  if you had to pick a general that's in  the united states army today that's your  kind of general you got anybody i got  lots of them name a couple oh sure  there's jim musselman johnny howard  jim bukiyama  kids that i knew who were young  lieutenants young majors and so on don  hilbert right here in your city they've  got a lot of  very confident guys i'm very pleased  with the  number of the generals that i know that  i've soldiered with glenn mallory i  could and i could name probably a dozen  so i think i think that the  somebody who's picking generals or  picking the kind of guys who will stand  in the door and who'll give us  uh who bring back that warrior leader  spirit that we had in this country  before the patents and the ridgeways  back in 1971 you went on issues and  answers  to say  had you planned to say the strong things  about the vietnam war that you did  well  i had  a lot of time to ruminate about it and  it was a hell of a decision because i  knew i was cutting my throat in terms of  the military i knew i was all washed up  if i sounded off but i felt it was  necessary that i did and so i did my  homework and i  mean  i knew i was going to talk about  training i was going to talk about  vietnamization i was going to talk about  how the war was being fought about the  bad senior leadership and  so  when it came to to  filming it i i had my  act together where'd you do it right in  vietnam in the mekong delta you remember  who interviewed you  yeah a guy named howard tuchner from abc  and his uh  nick george  who was the abc bureau chief who later  and nick george was very responsible for  i think  uh  probably keeping me alive because the  army became immediately angry and a lot  of funny things happened to me and uh  nick george just simply assigned a  camera team and a and a uh  narrator to me and wherever i went along  with this abc team and i had the power  of the press it was a great insurance  policy in my back pocket did they know  